 Run `python3 -m unittest` and `python3 bench.py` from this directory.
 
+
+## Throughput estimate
+
+`bench.py` prepares small, valid JSON lines in memory, warms up with five
+1,000-record batches, then times 300 batches through `feed.import_lines`.
+Each batch has new IDs and is committed to a fresh file-backed SQLite database;
+the final row count is checked. Input generation, opening the database and
+network or feed delivery are outside the timing. The mean batch time is
+compared with the one second available for 1,000 records at 1,000 records/s:
+margin = 1 second minus mean processing time. The p95 and maximum are batch
+latencies in this short run, not guarantees. The benchmark also prints the
+measured throughput.
+
+On the test machine (Python 3.13.5, Linux, temporary directory on tmpfs),
+four runs measured 2.980–4.196 ms per 1,000 records, leaving
+995.804–997.020 ms (99.58–99.70%) of each second. This suggests ample
+capacity for this particular workload. The runs execute back-to-back, so they
+simulate 300 seconds of arrivals but do not establish performance over 300
+seconds of wall-clock time. The temporary filesystem is memory-backed: these
+measurements do not represent persistent disk latency or durability. Larger
+records, invalid records, an existing larger database, different batch sizes,
+concurrent work, network delivery and sustained operation can change the
+result. Measure again with representative data, storage and a longer run for
+production planning.
